When the API uses `undefined` to signal the end, the last page sets the cursor to `undefined`, and the condition `undefined !== null` evaluates to `true`. The loop keeps running and calls `fetch(undefined)` — typically re-requesting the first page (many APIs treat a missing cursor param as "first page"), so it loops forever re-fetching page 1, or the server rejects the request. Either way, it never terminates.

