## The bug

The loop assumes the only "no more pages" signal is `null`:

```js
while (nextCursor !== null) { fetch(nextCursor) }
```

When the API uses `undefined` to signal the end, the last page sets the cursor to `undefined`, and the condition `undefined !== null` evaluates to `true`. The loop keeps running and calls `fetch(undefined)` — typically re-requesting the first page (many APIs treat a missing cursor param as "first page"), so it loops forever re-fetching page 1, or the server rejects the request. Either way, it never terminates.

Note the inverse failure too: if the API ever switched to `null`, a check for `undefined` would break the same way. Sentinel-sensitive comparisons are fragile.

## Robust termination condition

Test for "no usable cursor" rather than one specific sentinel value — a next-page cursor is a non-empty string when present, so treat anything falsy as the end:

```js
let cursor; // undefined → first page
do {
  const page = await fetch(cursor);
  process(page.items);
  cursor = page.nextCursor;
} while (cursor != null && cursor !== "");
```

Key points:

- `cursor != null` covers both `undefined` and `null` (loose equality) without catching other falsy-but-valid values the way `!cursor` would.
- `cursor !== ""` additionally guards against APIs that return an empty string for "no next page".
- Add a hard cap as a safety net against silent API contract changes that would otherwise cause an infinite loop:

```js
let cursor;
let pageCount = 0;
const MAX_PAGES = 100;
do {
  const page = await fetch(cursor);
  process(page.items);
  cursor = page.nextCursor;
  if (++pageCount > MAX_PAGES) throw new Error("Pagination exceeded MAX_PAGES");
} while (cursor != null && cursor !== "");
```

Optionally, normalize the sentinel once at the response boundary (`const hasMore = cursor != null && cursor !== ""`), so loop logic doesn't depend on the raw API convention at all.
